\section*{Unique Games Approximation-Threshold Conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that for any fixed positive completeness and soundness errors, a deterministic polynomial reduction from 3SAT produces fixed-alphabet Unique Games with the requested gap.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For every fixed $\varepsilon,\delta\in(0,1/2)$, the claim gives $s\ge1$ and a deterministic polynomial-time $3$SAT reduction to explicit unweighted Unique Games over $K=\mathbb F_2^s$, on a simple bipartite graph with translation constraints $a(v_e)=a(u_e)+c_e$, satisfying
\[
 \varphi\text{ satisfiable}\Rightarrow\operatorname{val}(G_{\varphi})\ge1-\varepsilon,
 \qquad\varphi\text{ unsatisfiable}\Rightarrow\operatorname{val}(G_{\varphi})\le\delta.
\]
Alphabet size and polynomial constants depend only on the two fixed errors. For any fixed constraint-language instance $J$ with $A=\operatorname{OPT}(J)<B=\operatorname{SDP}_{R}(J)$, the associated consequence claims NP-hardness of distinguishing value at least $B-\eta$ from value at most $A+\eta$ for any fixed $0<\eta<(B-A)/2$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), a latent quadratic alphabet and matrix-slice tests supply an outer permutation game. High acceptance decodes agreeing advice strategies, whereas clean-coordinate estimates from the unsatisfiable parity instance force their agreement to decay, giving the soundness contradiction.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The Unique Games result supplies hardness and the finite-CSP SDP characterization. Computing or approximating the threshold itself is an additional algorithmic task not established by the hardness statement.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: for any fixed positive completeness and soundness errors, a deterministic polynomial reduction from 3SAT produces fixed-alphabet Unique Games with the requested gap.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{DirectedFeedback.lean}, \texttt{MinUncut.lean}, \texttt{OptimalMaxCut.lean}, \texttt{UniqueGamesTheorem.lean}, \texttt{VertexCover.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.DirectedFeedback.main}, \texttt{OAI.MinUncut.main}, \texttt{OAI.OptimalMaxCut.main}, \texttt{OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.theorem11}, \texttt{OAI.VertexCover.every\_fixed\_factor\_below\_two}, \texttt{OAI.VertexCover.explicit\_cover\_gap}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

